  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The wrinkled bolete is a medium-sized fungus characterized by its distinctive wrinkled or reticulated cap surface and yellow pores that bruise blue-green when touched. It belongs to the bolete family (Boletaceae) and is commonly found throughout New England during summer and fall months.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Wrinkled Cap Surface",
      "description": "The cap has a distinctive wrinkled or reticulated (net-like) texture that is highly diagnostic for this species",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Yellow Pores with Blue-Green Bruising",
      "description": "Yellow pores on the underside of the cap that quickly turn blue-green when pressed or damaged",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Red-Tinged Stem Base",
      "description": "The stem typically has reddish or pinkish coloration, especially toward the base",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Wrinkled boletes fruit on the forest floor in deciduous and mixed woodlands, often near oak and other hardwood trees in moist conditions.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The blue-green bruising reaction occurs because the bolete contains compounds that oxidize when cell walls are damaged, creating this distinctive color change that helps distinguish it from similar species.",
